			The thread boils down to a familiar split. One side sees LLM use as inevitable, a tool that lowers the barrier to entry. The other side sees it as a shortcut that undermines the very point of the hobby. Neither side is entirely wrong, and neither side is listening.

			Hobby programming has always been about the process, not just the output. The struggle to debug, to understand, to finally get it working — that is the reward. When an LLM hands you the answer, you skip the struggle. You also skip the learning. That is a legitimate loss, and dismissing it as nostalgia helps no one.

			But the trend line is real. Tools change what a community values. That does not mean every change is good, and it does not mean resistance is pointless. It means the community has to decide what it wants to preserve, and say so clearly instead of sneering.

