			Ah yes, the unstoppable trend line. I've seen this exact chart three times before: expert systems in '87, big data in '11, blockchain in '16. Every time, the demos were jaw-dropping, the keynote slides were flawless, and the production deployments were smoldering craters. The LLM boosters keep telling us that 'the barrier to entry is lower' — sure, if you consider generating code that fails in production at 4 AM a barrier being lowered. Show me the prod deployment that survived a year. Show me the maintenance cost. Show me the vendor who didn't quietly retract their 'AI-first' roadmap six months later. I'll wait.

			As for the hobbyist communities — they're not scared of the future, they're just not stupid. They've read the HN threads and seen the same pattern: a beginner pipes some boilerplate through Copilot, it looks great, then it breaks in some non-obvious way that requires actual understanding to fix. The 'born against' crowd isn't against tools; they're against the idea that you can skip learning the craft. I remember when 'Stack Overflow copy-paste' was the lazy shortcut that old-timers mocked. Now we're supposed to believe that copy-pasting from a statistical parrot is enlightenment? Spare me.

			The trend line isn't unstoppable — it's episodic. Hype cycles don't die because people are skeptical; they die because reality catches up. Hobbyist communities are the canary in the coal mine: low budget, high stakes, zero patience for vaporware. When a junior dev using GPT-5 ships a project that his teammates can actually maintain and debug without summoning an exorcist, then I'll reconsider. Until then, I'll keep my skeptical eyes on the production logs, and I'll keep laughing at the 'AI will change everything' posts that get quietly deleted six months later.
